From: waveydude <23585435+waveydude@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] radv/ps5: RADV_PS5_SCRATCH_WAVES_PER_CU sets the scratch waves a compute unit

Unset, 32 as before. The scratch rings are waves times bytes a wave; at 32 a CU one compute
shader made a 128 MiB ring on the console.

---
diff --git a/src/amd/vulkan/radv_device.c b/src/amd/vulkan/radv_device.c
--- a/src/amd/vulkan/radv_device.c
+++ b/src/amd/vulkan/radv_device.c
@@ -1642,6 +1642,20 @@
     */
    uint32_t max_threads_per_block = 2048;
    device->scratch_waves = MAX2(32 * pdev->info.num_cu, max_threads_per_block / 64);
+   {
+      /* EglantinePS5: RADV_PS5_SCRATCH_WAVES_PER_CU=<1..31> in env.txt (unset: 32, as before). The
+       * scratch rings are waves x bytes a wave, and on the console one compute shader's scratch
+       * made a 128 MiB ring (RADV_PS5_MEMORY rings=137984 KiB, the largest 131072) out of the pool
+       * the CPU and the GPU share, in a game that ran out of it (Ocarina of Time, Castle Town). The
+       * comment above recommends 4 a CU at most and measured ~2% between 4 and 32. Never under one
+       * thread group's waves, so the hardware can always start one. */
+      const unsigned long asked = (unsigned long)debug_get_num_option("RADV_PS5_SCRATCH_WAVES_PER_CU", 0);
+      if (asked >= 1 && asked < 32) {
+         device->scratch_waves = MAX2((uint32_t)asked * pdev->info.num_cu, max_threads_per_block / 64);
+         fprintf(stderr, "radv/ps5: scratch waves %u (%lu a CU of %u)\n", device->scratch_waves, asked,
+                 pdev->info.num_cu);
+      }
+   }
 
    device->dispatch_initiator = S_00B800_COMPUTE_SHADER_EN(1);
 
